"# Research Report: Wikipedia as a Source — Reliability, Editorial Model, and Citation Practice\n\n**Note on scope:** The session did not carry forward an explicit question statement, so this report addresses the core subject implied by the request — whether and how Wikipedia can be used and cited as a source — synthesizing retrieved Wikipedia policy/guideline pages and the academic literature they and related sources document. This inference is stated as a limitation in Section 6.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Wikipedia's Editorial Model\n\nWikipedia (launched 15 January 2001) is self-governing: content decisions rest with the editing community, not the Wikimedia Foundation. Its \"Five Pillars\" establish it as an encyclopedia written from a **neutral point of view**, with free content, respectful conduct, and no firm rules [1].\n\nThree core content policies govern quality [2][3][4]:\n\n- **Neutral Point of View (NPOV):** all significant published views must be represented fairly and proportionately, weighted by prominence in reliable sources.\n- **Verifiability:** challenged material must be attributable to a reliable published source — summarized as \"verifiability, not truth.\"\n- **No Original Research:** no unpublished data, arguments, or synthesis of sources to imply unstated conclusions.\n\nQuality control operates through layered mechanisms: permanent edit histories, watchlists and recent-changes patrol, machine-learning anti-vandalism tools (**ClueBot NG**, **ORES**), the Edit Filter (2009), tiered page protection (semi-, extended-confirmed, full), deletion processes, and formal quality tiers — **Good Articles** (40,000+) and **Featured Articles** (6,000+ on English Wikipedia) [5][6]. Governance escalates from talk pages through Requests for Comment to the 15-member **Arbitration Committee**, which issues binding rulings [7].\n\n## 2. Evidence on Reliability\n\nThe landmark 2005 *Nature* study compared 42 science article pairs against Encyclopædia Britannica: 8 serious errors (4 each), and total inaccuracies of 162 (Wikipedia) vs. 123 (Britannica) — roughly 4 vs. 3 errors per article. *Nature* concluded Wikipedia \"comes close to Britannica\" in science accuracy [8]. Britannica's 2006 \"Fatally Flawed\" rebuttal challenged the excerpting method and error definitions; *Nature* declined to retract and released its review data [9].\n\nLater domain-specific studies broadly corroborate comparable accuracy with weaknesses in **completeness and depth**: Rosenzweig (2006) and Rector (2008) found Wikipedia history articles comparable to Encarta and slightly below Britannica, with superior breadth [10][11]; Reavley et al. (2012) found mental-health content roughly as accurate as Britannica [12]. Medical content warrants more caution: Kräenbring et al. (2014) found ~80% of drug statements accurate but with significant omissions [13]; Hasty et al. (2014) found 9 of 10 articles on the costliest US medical conditions contained statements discordant with peer-reviewed literature [14]. Greenstein & Zhu found political slant declined over time until Wikipedia was comparable to or less biased than Britannica [15].\n\n## 3. Criticisms and Known Limitations\n\n- **Vandalism and hoaxes:** a small but persistent share of edits is damaging; most is reverted within minutes, but hoaxes have survived long periods — the Seigenthaler biography hoax (132 days, 2005), the \"Bicholim Conflict\" (5 years, and briefly a \"Good Article\"), and fabricated Maurice Jarre obituary quotes picked up by major newspapers [16][17].\n- **Systemic bias and the gender gap:** editors skew heavily male (roughly 80–90%) and Global North; women's biographies are shorter and more often missing, though the share has risen from ~15% (2014) to ~19% in the early 2020s [18][19]. Emblematic incidents include the Donna Strickland (2018) and Clarice Phelps (2019) article rejections/deletions [20].\n- **Coverage gaps:** geotagged content massively overrepresents Europe and North America; Africa holds only ~2–3% of geotagged articles despite ~15% of world population [21]. Bot-generated stubs (e.g., Lsjbot) inflate article counts — notably making Cebuano Wikipedia the second-largest edition — without commensurate human quality control [22].\n- **Ideological-slant disputes:** a 2024 Manhattan Institute analysis found measurable leftward drift in article language (2011–2022); co-founder Larry Sanger has alleged left-leaning bias, which Jimmy Wales disputes [23].\n\n## 4. Wikipedia as a Citable Source\n\nWikipedia is a **tertiary source** — it summarizes primary and secondary literature rather than presenting original research. Notably, **Wikipedia itself states it is not a reliable source** and should not be cited as an authority: its own guidance (\"Wikipedia:Citing Wikipedia,\" \"Wikipedia:Academic use,\" \"Wikipedia:Researching with Wikipedia\") directs researchers to use articles as starting points, verify claims against the article's footnoted sources, and cite those underlying sources instead [24][25][26]. If citation is unavoidable (e.g., when the fact being cited concerns Wikipedia itself), the recommended practice is to cite the **permanent link (old revision)** with date and time [24].\n\nStyle guides reflect this hierarchy: APA (7th ed.) permits citation but calls it generally inappropriate in academic writing; Chicago discourages it and recommends footnote-only use; MLA provides a template while advising use of underlying sources [27][28]. Courts have cautioned against reliance on Wikipedia [29], and clinical guidance treats it as unsuitable for medical decision-making [30].\n\n**Best practice:** use Wikipedia for background and orientation; mine its references; verify load-bearing claims independently; cite the original sources — or, if necessary, the specific permanent revision.\n\n## 5. Bottom Line\n\nThe best-supported conclusion is that Wikipedia's factual accuracy in general and scientific topics has been **broadly comparable to traditional encyclopedias** since 2005, with weaknesses concentrated in completeness, prose quality, coverage gaps, and medical depth rather than outright error rates. It is best treated as a high-quality tertiary index and research gateway — not a citable authority — and reliability varies by topic, language edition, and article maturity.\n\n## 6. Limitations and Uncertainties\n\n1. **Inferred topic.** The original question was not explicitly stated in this session; the topic was inferred from the instruction to cite retrieved Wikipedia sources. If the intended question differed, this report may not address it.\n2. **Snippet-only retrieval.** Full-page fetching was disabled; findings rest on search snippets and research-agent summaries, so some details (exact percentages, dates, page titles) could not be verified against live pages.\n3. **Unverified secondary references.** Academic citations (e.g., *Nature* 2005, Hasty 2014, Greenstein & Zhu) are widely documented but were not independently re-fetched; readers should consult the originals.\n4. **Moving target.** Wikipedia articles, policies, and statistics change continuously; editor counts, gender-gap figures, and article tallies are approximate and date-dependent (as of the retrieved 2024–2025-era data).\n5. **Generalizability.** Reliability studies use small, often non-random samples (42 articles in *Nature*; 10 conditions in Hasty), inconsistent error definitions, and domain skew toward medicine; results may not transfer to other topics or language editions.\n6. **Contested claims.** Ideological-slant findings (Rozado 2024; Sanger vs. Wales) are actively disputed and presented here as claims, not established fact.\n\n---\n\n### Sources\n\n[1] Wikipedia, \"Wikipedia:Five Pillars.\" [2] Wikipedia, \"Wikipedia:Neutral point of view.\" [3] Wikipedia, \"Wikipedia:Verifiability.\" [4] Wikipedia, \"Wikipedia:No original research.\" [5] Wikipedia, \"Wikipedia:Protection policy\"; \"Wikipedia:Edit filter.\" [6] Wikipedia, \"Wikipedia:Content assessment\"; \"Wikipedia:Good article criteria.\" [7] Wikipedia, \"Wikipedia:Arbitration Committee.\" [8] Giles, J., \"Internet encyclopaedias go head to head,\" *Nature* 438:900–901 (2005). [9] Encyclopædia Britannica, \"Fatally Flawed\" (2006); *Nature* editorial response (2006). [10] Rosenzweig, R., *Journal of American History* 93(1) (2006). [11] Rector, L.H., *Reference Services Review* 36(1) (2008). [12] Reavley et al., *ANZJP* 46(2) (2012). [13] Kräenbring et al., *PLoS ONE* 9(9) (2014). [14] Hasty et al., *JAOA* 114(5) (2014). [15] Greenstein & Zhu, *Management Science* 64(7) (2018). [16] Seigenthaler, J., *USA Today* (2005). [17] Coverage of the Bicholim Conflict hoax and Jarre obituary vandalism (2009, 2012). [18] Wikimedia Foundation Editor Surveys (2011–2021). [19] Wagner et al., \"It's a Man's Wikipedia?,\" ICWSM (2015); Humaniki/Wikidata metrics. [20] Coverage of Strickland (2018) and Phelps (2019) incidents. [21] Graham, M. et al., Oxford Internet Institute geotagging studies (2011). [22] Coverage of Lsjbot (2014–15). [23] Rozado, D., Manhattan Institute report (2024); public statements by Sanger and Wales. [24] Wikipedia, \"Wikipedia:Citing Wikipedia.\" [25] Wikipedia, \"Wikipedia:Academic use.\" [26] Wikipedia, \"Wikipedia:Researching with Wikipedia\"; \"Wikipedia:Identifying reliable sources.\" [27] APA, *Publication Manual*, 7th ed.; APA Style blog on citing Wikipedia. [28] *The Chicago Manual of Style*, 17th ed., §14.233; MLA Style Center guidance. [29] U.S. case law commentary (e.g., *United States v. Lawson*, 3d Cir. 2008). [30] Professional/clinical guidance on Wikipedia in medicine."